 ## Overview
-The Parametric Method is an algorithm for generating a 3D line segment primitive by computing points along the segment using a parameter $t$, then mapping these points to voxels [S1, unofficial]. It is identified as having the best performance characteristics in terms of speed and potential for parallelization in GPGPU implementation compared to other methods like the seven-neighbor search [S1, unofficial].
+Parametric Line Segment Voxelization is a method for generating a 3D line segment primitive by computing points along the segment using a parameter $t$, then mapping these points to voxels [S1, unofficial]. It solves the problem of efficiently decomposing a continuous line segment into a discrete set of voxels suitable for volume visualization. It is distinguished from sibling methods like the seven-neighbor search by its independence of voxel calculations; each voxel's position is computed directly from the segment parameters, making it highly parallelizable on GPGPU architectures [S1, unofficial].
 
