+### Porous Voxelization
+A method for porous structures using stochastic filtering to probabilistically discard voxels. It differs from parametric methods by not relying on geometric continuity but on spatial distribution. [Porous Voxelization](4361-porous-voxelization.md)
+
+### Crosshairs Intersection Target Method
+A method for generating primitives by intersecting crosshairs. It differs from parametric methods by using intersection logic rather than direct parameterization. [Crosshairs Intersection Target Method](4608-crosshairs-intersection-target-method.md)
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N)$ per segment, where $N$ is the length of the segment in voxels. For multiple segments, it is proportional to the sum of lengths, but GPU implementation may pad to the maximum length, leading to $O(N_{max} \times M)$ where $M$ is the number of segments if not handled carefully [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ to store the output voxel array for a single segment.
-*   **Performance:** The method possesses the best performance characteristics in terms of speed and potential for parallelization in GPGPU implementation compared to the "seven neighboring voxels-applicants" method [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Parallelization:** The search for a certain voxel does not depend on the search for neighboring voxels, allowing for high parallelization potential [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** High speed, excellent parallelization suitability for GPU, simple computation.
-*   **Limitations:** Redundant computation for short segments in batch processing; branch divergence in GPU kernels if segment lengths vary significantly [S1, unofficial].
-*   **When to Use:** When generating 3D line segments for volume visualization on GPU, especially when performance and parallelization are critical [S1, unofficial].
-*   **When Not to Use:** When memory bandwidth is extremely constrained and segment lengths vary wildly, causing significant padding overhead; or when exact topological connectivity (like the 7-neighbor method might provide) is required for subsequent processing steps that depend on neighbor relationships [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N)$ per segment, where $N$ is the integer length of the segment.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ to store the resulting voxel array.
+*   **Performance:** The method possesses the best performance characteristics in terms of speed and potential for parallelization in GPGPU implementation compared to other methods like the seven-neighbor search [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Parallelization:** The search for a certain voxel does not depend on the search for neighboring voxels, allowing for high parallelization on GPUs [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** For multiple segments with different lengths, the method may suffer from redundant computations and branch divergence if not carefully implemented on the GPU [S1, unofficial].
+*   **When to Use:** Use for GPGPU generation of 3D line segments for volume visualization in 3D graphics systems where speed and parallelization are critical [S1, unofficial].
+*   **When Not to Use:** Avoid if the scene contains many short segments with highly variable lengths and strict memory constraints, as the fixed-block allocation strategy may lead to inefficiency.
 
 ## Background
-*   **Origin:** The method is part of a generalized method for generating arbitrary 3D volume graphics primitives $\Gamma(x,y,z)$ bounded by surface $P(x,y,z)$ and curve $\Phi(x,y,z)$ [S1, unofficial].
-*   **History:** Developed and theoretically investigated by Anas M. Al-Oraiqat and Sergii A. Zori [S1, unofficial]. It was evaluated against other algorithms like the "seven neighboring voxels-applicants" method [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Usage:** Used for GPGPU generation of 3D line segments for volume visualization in 3D graphics systems [S1, unofficial]. Experiments were conducted on NVIDIA GPUs including GeForce GTX 260, 1050, 760, and 1070i [S1, unofficial].
+The Parametric Method was proposed by Anas M. Al-Oraiqat and Sergii A. Zori in their paper "3D Primitives Gpgpu Generation for Volume Visualization in 3D Graphics Systems" [S1, unofficial]. The authors evaluated and compared several 3D Graphics Primitive generation algorithms for straight segments, concluding that the Parametric Method offers superior efficiency and parallelization potential for GPGPU implementation compared to methods like the seven-neighbor search [S1, unofficial]. The method is based on a generalized approach for generating arbitrary 3D volume primitives [S1, unofficial].
 
